            "<strong>Holding a child still</strong> for a procedure is a clinical skill, not force. Use the minimum hold for the shortest time, explain it to the child and parent, prefer a comforting position (the child sitting on the parent's lap facing her, chest to chest, with one arm held out) to pinning on a couch, never use a hold as punishment, and stop and reassess if the child is becoming exhausted or terrified — a child fighting through a lumbar puncture is unsafe for everyone. <strong>Pain</strong> in children is under-recognised and under-treated: assess it with the right tool — <strong>FLACC</strong> (face, legs, activity, cry, consolability, each scored 0–2) for the pre-verbal child from about two months to seven years and for any child who cannot self-report; the <strong>Wong–Baker faces</strong> scale from about three years; a <strong>numerical 0–10 scale</strong> from about eight years; and a neonatal scale such as NIPS for newborns — and treat it by the clock with paracetamol, ibuprofen and, when needed, oral morphine (Lesson 7.1), alongside the non-drug measures that work: the parent, sucking, swaddling, distraction, warmth and position.",
