            "<strong>Family-centred care</strong> recognises that the family is the constant in the child's life and the hospital a brief interruption; the nurse's role is to support and teach the family, not to replace it. In practice this means the parent is a partner in care — present on the round, told the plan in plain language, taught every skill she will need (giving medicines with a syringe, feeding by cup, cleaning a wound, counting breaths), and asked about what she notices, because she notices first. It means respecting the family's culture and beliefs and negotiating rather than dismissing: the grandmother who wants to apply something to the cord can be shown chlorhexidine gel and why; the father who distrusts the hospital can be brought to the bedside. It means noticing the <strong>siblings</strong> at home — frightened, neglected, sometimes blamed by themselves for the illness — and encouraging visits where the ward allows and honest information where it does not.",
            "The <strong>parents of a child with a disability or chronic illness</strong> carry a load that is physical, financial, social and spiritual: a child with cerebral palsy to carry and feed, the cost of transport to a distant clinic, neighbours who say the child is a curse or a 'spirit child', a marriage under strain, and their own grief for the child they expected. They are experts in their own child; listen to them, give information in portions they can absorb, connect them to the Department of Social Welfare, disability associations, community-based rehabilitation and the LEAP cash transfer where eligible, and never speak of the child in front of her as if she were not there (Lesson 6.2).",
